/home/ubuntu/aklt/manuscript/bundle/manuscript/sec4-mixing-to-gap.tex:70:\begin{lemma}\label{l:shellgeom}
/home/ubuntu/aklt/manuscript/bundle/manuscript/sec4-mixing-to-gap.tex:101:\emph{Step $i$.} Let $2 \le i \le i_1$. If $i \in I_0$, the pair is not changed. Otherwise we apply a surgery on $F_i$, with a kernel $T_i$ defined as follows. The cells within distance $1$ of $F_i$ lie in $F_i$, in $D_{i - 1}$, or outside $\mathcal C_M$ at distance more than $(i - 1)s - 1 \ge 1$ from $y$, where $\tau$ and $\tau'$ agree. Hence if $\omega_{D_{i - 1}} = \omega'_{D_{i - 1}}$, then $\hat\mu^{\omega\tau}_{F_i} = \hat\mu^{\omega'\tau'}_{F_i}$, and $T_i$ resamples $F_i$ from this measure with the same configuration for both components. If $\omega_{D_{i - 1}} \neq \omega'_{D_{i - 1}}$, $T_i$ first draws the two configurations on $B_i$ from a maximal coupling of the marginals $\hat\mu^{\omega\tau}_{F_i, B_i}$ and $\hat\mu^{\omega'\tau'}_{F_i, B_i}$, then completes each to $F_i$ independently from the conditional laws given $B_i$, and finally replaces the configurations on $A_i$ by independent samples from their conditional laws given the rest. Each of these operations preserves the laws $\hat\mu^{\omega\tau}_{F_i}$ and $\hat\mu^{\omega'\tau'}_{F_i}$. By \eqref{e:W}, the two configurations agree on $B_i$ with probability at least $p_0$ (if $i \le s$). The last operation does not change $B_i$, and for every configuration $\sigma$ of $A_i$ the conditional probability of $\sigma$ given the rest is at least $\alpha^{|A_i|}$, by the chain rule and finite energy. Hence, by Lemma~\ref{l:shellgeom}, the two configurations agree on $D_i = A_i \cup B_i$ after step $i$ with probability at least $p_0 \alpha^{2 |A_i|} \ge \delta$, conditionally on any configuration before step $i$.
/home/ubuntu/aklt/manuscript/bundle/manuscript/sec4-mixing-to-gap.tex:138:The proof of Theorem~\ref{t:main} uses results of \S\ref{s:cut}--\S\ref{s:computer}; their proofs do not use \S\ref{s:frames}--\S\ref{s:mixing}, so the order of the argument is not circular.
/home/ubuntu/aklt/manuscript/bundle/manuscript/sec5-cut.tex:38:Let $D \subset \Z^2$ be finite, let $(D_x, w_x)$ be a frame (Definition~\ref{d:frame}) for every $x \in D$, and let $\Phi(n)$ be a product of factors $1 + n_x \cdot v$, where $x \in D$ and $v$ is either a spin $n_y$, $y \in D$, or a fixed unit vector, such that every $x \in D$ appears in at most four factors. Then
/home/ubuntu/aklt/manuscript/bundle/manuscript/sec5-cut.tex:45:Order the sites of $D$ as $x_1, \dots, x_p$ and replace the sum over $n_{x_j}$ by the integral, one site at a time. At step $j$ the summand is a polynomial of degree at most $4$ in $n_{x_j}$, whose coefficients are functions of the spins summed or integrated at the other steps; integrating out other spins does not raise the degree in $n_{x_j}$. By Definition~\ref{d:frame}, the sum over $n_{x_j} \in D_{x_j}$ with weights $w_{x_j}$ equals the integral, and Fubini's theorem completes the induction.
/home/ubuntu/aklt/manuscript/bundle/manuscript/sec5-cut.tex:138:We now construct, for the sets $F_i$ and $B_i$ of \eqref{e:shells}, a cut to which Lemma~\ref{l:cut} and Lemma~\ref{l:removal} apply with a cost bounded uniformly in $M$. A cut that closes around the ends of the shell segment $B_i$ must pass between these ends and the boundary of the square, at a distance of order $l_0$ from the pins; to keep the number of such cut sites bounded, the cut widens linearly away from the boundary of the square.
/home/ubuntu/aklt/manuscript/bundle/manuscript/sec5-cut.tex:179:It remains to count the cells of the second case with a given $t = j$. Such a cell lies on the $\ell^\infty$ sphere of radius $k \pm j$ around $y$ and on the ring $\{c : h(c) = h'\}$ for one of at most three integers $h' \in [l_0/2 + j - 2, l_0/2 + j)$. Both are boundaries of axis-parallel squares, so they meet in at most $8$ cells unless a side of the sphere lies on a side of the ring, that is, unless a side of the sphere of radius $k \pm j$ that meets $\mathcal C_M$ is parallel to an edge $\mathcal E$ of $\mathcal C_M$ at distance exactly $h'$ from the half-plane beyond $\mathcal E$. When the radius changes from $k$ to $k \pm j$, the distance of such a side changes by $j$. By Lemma~\ref{l:shellgeom}, the sides of the sphere of radius $k$ that are parallel to an edge and meet $\mathcal C_M$ are at distance at least $s/3$; those of radius $k \pm j$ are therefore at distance at least $s/3 - j \ge s/3 - s/12 > l_0/2 + j$ when $s$ is large, or they come from a side of radius $k$ that does not meet $\mathcal C_M$ (distance at most $0$), and then their distance is at most $j < l_0/2 + j - 2$. In both cases it differs from $h'$. Hence there are at most $2 \cdot 3 \cdot 8 = 48$ cells, that is, at most $192$ sites, with $t = j$, and their contribution is at most
